<!-- SECTION: receipts.Receipts — title -->
<div class="titleband" data-screen-label="Receipts — title"><h1>The <span class="u-1">Receipts</span></h1></div>
<div class="introband">
  <h2>Every number, out in the open.</h2>
  <p>The Receipts is the methodology and source layer under everything we publish. The rule is simple: every number has a source, every source has a date, and every model states its assumptions. If a claim can&rsquo;t be sourced, we label it — plainly — as an estimate, a scenario, or a hypothesis, not a fact.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION: receipts.The register -->
<section class="blk blk--wheat" id="register" data-screen-label="The register">
  <div class="eyebrow">The register</div>
  <h2 class="u-129 sec">Every figure we publish, with its receipt.</h2>
  <p class="u-130 lead">{{ register.stats | length }} sourced figures, each with the publisher, the exact title, the date we last checked it and the region it covers. Nothing here is illustrative — every number is traceable to a named public source.</p>
{#- P6 keyword filter. Ships hidden and is revealed by /js/keyword-filter.js,
    so with JS off a reader never sees a control that does nothing and the full
    register renders exactly as before. Not inside a <form>: nothing submits and
    nothing is fetched.

    The filter indexes ALL register cards, not just the ones currently paged in
    by /js/show-more.js below, so a query always searches the whole register. -#}
  <div class="rfilter" data-keyword-filter data-filter-for="register-cards" data-filter-noun="figures" hidden>
    <label for="rfilter-q">Filter these figures</label>
    <input id="rfilter-q" type="search" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Try &ldquo;rent&rdquo;, &ldquo;BLS&rdquo; or &ldquo;2026&rdquo;&hellip;">
    <span class="rfilter-count" data-filter-count></span>
  </div>
  <div class="cards" id="register-cards">
{% for row in register.stats %}
    {{ fig.card(row) }}
{% endfor %}
  </div>
{#- P6/R show-more. Every card is rendered server-side; /js/show-more.js pages
    the grid down to the first 12 and reveals this control, exactly the same
    contract as the filter bar above — with JS off all of them render and this
    control is never shown. The wrapper carries `hidden` (and no class that
    sets `display`, so the UA's [hidden] rule is not outranked); the button
    inside is a .u-btn, which does set display, and must never carry `hidden`
    itself. See css-R.css for the belt-and-braces stylesheet rule. -#}
  <p class="u-75" data-show-more data-show-more-for="register-cards" data-show-more-step="12" hidden>
    <button type="button" class="u-btn u-btn--ghost" aria-controls="register-cards">Show more receipts</button>
  </p>
  <p class="u-131">Figures are re-checked on a published cadence. A figure past its review date is relabelled <b>Needs refresh</b> rather than quietly left standing, and every change is recorded in the <a class="u-132" href="#corrections">corrections log</a>.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION: receipts.The rule -->
<section class="blk blk--white" data-screen-label="The rule">
  <div class="eyebrow">The rule, three ways</div>
  <h2 class="sec">If we can&rsquo;t show where a number came from, we don&rsquo;t print it.</h2>
  <div class="u-25 rule3">
    <div><div class="n">01</div><h3>Sourced</h3><p>Every figure carries a source and a license, so anyone can trace it back and reuse it lawfully.</p></div>
    <div><div class="n">02</div><h3>Dated</h3><p>Every source shows when it was captured and when it was last reviewed. Stale data is flagged, not hidden.</p></div>
    <div><div class="n">03</div><h3>Labelled</h3><p>Every figure carries a confidence tag, and anything hypothetical is marked illustrative — unmissably.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION: receipts.Keeping current -->
<section class="blk blk--ink u-block--ink" data-screen-label="Keeping current">
  <div class="u-111 eyebrow">Because data goes stale</div>
  <h2 class="u-133 sec">Built to stay <span class="hl-on-ink">current</span>.</h2>
  <p class="u-134 lead">The dashboards pull from public sources where possible, show visible &ldquo;last updated&rdquo; dates, and follow a regular review cadence. Automating those data pipelines is a funding priority — precisely so the evidence stays fresh instead of decaying quietly.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION: receipts.Corrections -->
<section class="blk blk--cream" id="corrections" data-screen-label="Corrections">
  <div class="eyebrow">Corrections &amp; changelog</div>
  <h2 class="u-129 sec">We log our mistakes.</h2>
  <p class="u-133 lead">Every correction is recorded publicly with a date and a plain description of what changed and why. Nothing is quietly edited. The full version-controlled history lives in the public records.</p>
  <div class="u-135">
    <span class="status status--planned">No corrections logged yet</span>
    <p class="u-136">The corrections log is empty because nothing here has needed a correction yet &mdash; not because nothing&rsquo;s been published. Every figure on this page above already carries its source, date, and confidence label. The day one needs fixing, it&rsquo;ll be logged here: dated, with what changed and why.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="u-75"><a href="https://github.com/uncost-org/uncost" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="u-btn u-btn--ghost">View public records on GitHub</a></p>
</section>

